<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Graph Learning with Deficiencies</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over the years, various methods have been proposed to enhance GNN performance under weak information. Relation-aware models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlichtkrull et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vashishth et al., 2019</a>)</cite> extend GNN architectures by modeling relations to compensate for incomplete and noisy structures. ACM-GNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luan et al., 2022</a>)</cite> proposes an adaptive channel mixing strategy, which relieves homophilous edge sparsity. Structure refinement models such as GRCN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Franceschi et al., 2019</a>)</cite> mitigate fixed structures by dynamically revising graph topologies, benefiting scenarios characterized by sparse and spurious edges. While perturbation techniques such as DropEdge or feature masking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rong et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papp et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2022</a>)</cite> are effective in improving generalization and solving overfitting, semi-supervised and few-shot methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>)</cite> alleviate the problem of limited labels. DropMessage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> perturbs node-to-node communication for boosting robustness in message-passing. Likewise, feature propagation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rossi et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and imputation (e.g., Neighbor Mean) achieve strong performance with missing features. RSGNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2022</a>)</cite> tackles both edge and supervision deficiency by down-weighting noisy edges and densifying the graph to exploit scarce labels. Besides, unsupervised methods such as DGI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Veličković et al., 2018</a>)</cite> cope with label deficiency by learning without labels. However, these approaches address specific deficiencies, and their effectiveness under compound deficiencies remains underexplored.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>LLM-on-graph</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Emerging paradigms have proliferated through the integration of LLMs and graphs. LLM-as-Encoder paradigm leverages pretrained LLMs to transform textual attributes into semantically richer and higher-dimensional embeddings, compared to shallow embeddings (e.g., bag-of-words) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khoshraftar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, whereas LLM-as-Enhancer augments GNNs with LLM-generated contents. TAPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2023</a>)</cite> distills textual explanations from GPT into compact embeddings that improve node classification, while LLM4NG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> generates labeled nodes for augmenting graphs under label-limited few-shot settings. Similarly, Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> show that textual features derived from frozen LLMs enrich node representations under various training paradigms. LLM-only reasoning frameworks, including LLM-as-Predictor, bypass GNNs entirely and delegate reasoning to LLMs. It’s important to note that, as LLM-only reasoning frameworks such as GraphText <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and GraphLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> operate independently of graph encoders, their performance is not directly comparable to that of GNN-based approaches, lying outside our scope.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>GNN Robustness with LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To date, the potential of LLM-on-graph and investigations on GNN robustness with LLMs are still underexplored. The only related work, Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, mainly examines whether LLM-as-Encoder improves adversarial robustness. However, they focus on adversarial robustness where sophisticated attackers launch crafted perturbations, while our work emphasizes graph defect robustness commonly encountered in real-world applications, towards more practical implications. Besides, their perturbation scope is narrowly limited to structural adversarial attacks, with feature, node outage, and supervision attacks unstudied. Most importantly, LLMs cannot be the only augmentation solutions, and they are not systematically benchmarked against conventional GNN-based approaches in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>GNN Robustness against Graph Deficiencies</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand how graph deficiencies affect GNN performance, we evaluate a diverse set of baselines including GNN-based and recent LLM-enhanced techniques against various deficiencies.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1. </span>Node Classification with Text-attributed Graphs</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E},\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E},\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a text-attributed graph, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}=\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}=\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}\subseteq\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\subseteq\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are the sets of nodes and edges, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of node attributes. Each node <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is coupled with corresponding texts <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as descriptions. To process such textual information, a function <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is employed to encode each <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into a representation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}=\phi(t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}=\phi(t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding a node feature matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> stacking all embeddings. Structural information is captured by an adjacency matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\{0,1\}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\{0,1\}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if an edge exists. In the semi-supervised setting, a node subset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{L}\subset\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{L}\subset\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with ground-truth labels <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{i}\mid v_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{i}\mid v_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{Y}|=C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{Y}|=C</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the total number of classes. The remaining nodes <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{U}=\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{V}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{U}=\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{V}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> are unlabeled, and the final goal is to predict their labels by learning a mapping <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(\mathbf{A},\mathbf{X})\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(\mathbf{A},\mathbf{X})\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2. </span>GNN robustness against graph deficiencies</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In real-world scenarios, the observed graph <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\text{obs}}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}_{\text{obs}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\text{obs}}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}_{\text{obs}})</annotation></semantics></math> and node features <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\text{obs}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\text{obs}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are rarely ideal. First, supervision is commonly constrained to a small subset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{L}\subset\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{L}\subset\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, having known labels <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{i}\}_{v_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{i}\}_{v_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}_{L}|\ll|\mathcal{V}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}_{L}|\ll|\mathcal{V}|</annotation></semantics></math>. The structural observation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{obs}}\subset\mathcal{E}_{\text{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{obs}}\subset\mathcal{E}_{\text{true}}</annotation></semantics></math> leads to incomplete adjacency <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\text{obs}}\subset\mathbf{A}_{\text{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\text{obs}}\subset\mathbf{A}_{\text{true}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Feature vectors can be partially missing with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists j:x_{ij}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists j:x_{ij}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, available nodes may be insufficient for learning meaningful representations when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|</annotation></semantics></math> is small or sparsely connected. These deficiencies imply that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{A}_{\text{obs}},\mathbf{X}_{\text{obs}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{A}_{\text{obs}},\mathbf{X}_{\text{obs}})</annotation></semantics></math> deviates from the intact graph <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{A}_{\text{true}},\mathbf{X}_{\text{true}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{A}_{\text{true}},\mathbf{X}_{\text{true}})</annotation></semantics></math>. GNNs are therefore required to satisfy <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{A}_{\text{obs}},\mathbf{X}_{\text{obs}})\approx f(\mathbf{A}_{\text{true}},\mathbf{X}_{\text{true}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{A}_{\text{obs}},\mathbf{X}_{\text{obs}})\approx f(\mathbf{A}_{\text{true}},\mathbf{X}_{\text{true}})</annotation></semantics></math> to establish robustness for reliable node representations and classification.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01910v1/Exploration_fig.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/165;" width="476" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Accuracy under increasing attack intensities.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3. </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We categorize baselines introduced in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Related Work ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> according to graph deficiencies they directly or potentially mitigate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structural Deficiency:</span> RSGNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, GRCN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and ACM-GNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luan et al., 2022</a>)</cite> enhance graph structure robustness against missing or noisy edges.
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervision Deficiency:</span> DGI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Veličković et al., 2018</a>)</cite>,
RSGNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and LLM4NG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> cope with the scarcity of labeled data.
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature Deficiency:</span> Neighbor Mean, Feature Propagation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rossi et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, TAPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and TA_E <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2023</a>)</cite> improve robustness to missing or corrupted node features.
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Node Deficiency:</span> DropEdge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rong et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and DropMessage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> help to learn robust representations with part of the nodes missing, since over-fitting weakens the generalization on small graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also adopt Mistral-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Qwen-3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> following the LLM-as-Encoder paradigm as baselines. All of the above baselines in the empirical study are built on top of a standard GCN backbone.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.4. </span>Empirical Study Settings</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct our empirical analysis on the Cora dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2016</a>)</cite>.
To ensure fair and consistent comparisons, all base GNN models (GCN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kipf, 2016</a>)</cite>, GAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Veličković et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, and GraphSAGE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hamilton et al., 2017</a>)</cite>) are implemented with identical configurations, see Appendix Section <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Configurations of Baseline Methods ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for details. For all LLM-as-Enhancer baselines, Sentence-BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych, 2019</a>)</cite> is employed to encode LLM-generated contents.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01910v1/exploration_3D.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:295/236;" width="295" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>GCN Performance under compound deficiencies.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Empirical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Four types of deficiencies are injected on Cora before training the downstream GNN classifiers. We report the perturbation space: Structural Homophily Attack (SHA) with edge reduction ratio <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{0,0.5,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{0,0.5,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math>, Supervision Scarcity Attack (SSA) with labeled training ratio <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{0.6,0.4,0.2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{0.6,0.4,0.2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, Feature Drop Attack (FDA) with feature drop ratio <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{0,0.5,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{0,0.5,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Node Removal Attack (NRA) with node drop ratio <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{0,0.5,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{0,0.5,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Validation and test sets are fixed at 20%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis addresses:
(1) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How do compound deficiencies impact baselines</span> (Obs. 1-2)?
(2) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Does LLM-as-Enhancer provides performance and robustness gains worth their complexity</span> (Obs. 3-5)?
(3) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Do baselines exhibit uniform or deficiency-specific sensitivity</span> (Obs. 6)?</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation 1.</span> <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compound deficiencies induce amplified performance degradation.</span>
Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which aggregates the exhaustive results by summing intensities of individual attacks from full tables exemplified by Appendix Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix C Research Methods ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, shows that accuracy consistently decreases as attack intensity increases. This cumulative impact exceeds what any single deficiency would cause in isolation. Taking vanilla GCN for illustration, the decline in both its performance visualization Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1.4. Empirical Study Settings ‣ 3.1. Preliminaries ‣ 3. GNN Robustness against Graph Deficiencies ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> further becomes steeper and non-linear when multiple deficiencies co-occur at high levels, revealing a compounding effect that accelerates performance degradation. This necessitates jointly analyzing graph deficiencies rather than in isolation, a perspective rarely studied before.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation 2.</span> <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classic GNN is strong baseline.</span> Classic message-passing GNN (GCN) exhibits strong performance against almost all non-LLM baselines under compound graph deficiencies, which aligns with the findings of Luo et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Our results extend this evidence to robustness studies by demonstrating classic GCN’s competitiveness when evaluated with a large suite of attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation 3.</span> <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-as-Encoder exhibits superior robustness by providing richer high-dimensional embeddings.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Mistral and Qwen Embeddings sustain higher accuracy, showing only gradual declines under strong attacks. The exhibited robustness can be attributed to the richer and higher-dimensional embeddings that LLMs’ hidden layers provide. As LLM-based encoders use fundamentally different pretrained embeddings and are not included in subsequent comparisons for fairness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation 4.</span> <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM augmentations fall behind simple GNN counterparts under modest deficiencies.</span> Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1.2. GNN robustness against graph deficiencies ‣ 3.1. Preliminaries ‣ 3. GNN Robustness against Graph Deficiencies ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> show that across low-to-moderate attack regimes, both LLM-as-Encoder and LLM-as-Enhancer paradigms perform worse-to-comparable accuracy than much simpler GNN-based counterparts. This observation demonstrates that the integration of LLMs does not consistently yield better performance and can be even worse in many situations. Given the trade-offs mentioned in Section <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, especially compared with extreme naive approaches such as Feature Propagation, Neighbor Mean, and DropMessage, LLMs are not always efficient GNN helpers against graph deficiencies.</p>
</div>
