</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation 5.</span> <span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-as-Enhancer (e.g., LLM4NG, TAPE, TA_E) shows no clear robustness advantage over conventional GNN baselines.</span> While LLM Encoders achieve the highest robustness metrics in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2. Empirical Analysis ‣ 3. GNN Robustness against Graph Deficiencies ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, they operate on a fundamentally different representational basis. LLM-as-Enhancer baselines exhibit markedly weaker robustness. Their Worst Acc, Avg Acc, and Norm-AUC scores lag behind or show no improvement over a substantial set of conventional GNN approaches. This finding points out that despite their higher architectural complexity, current LLM-as-Enhancer frameworks fail to deliver tangible robustness gains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation 6.</span> <span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Different baselines exhibit distinct sensitivity profiles to deficiency types.</span>
On Cora, the examined baselines exhibit distinct vulnerability patterns. For instance, ACM-GNN, DropEdge, and Neighbor Mean are highly sensitive to NRA and SHA, showing steep performance degradation, but are less affected by FDA. In contrast, LLM4NG and TAPE are relatively more stable under FDA and SHA but show moderate sensitivity to high-intensity NRA. RS-GNN is relatively robust to FDA but performs poorly overall. Therefore, robustness is highly algorithm-dependent, and different baselines present disparate sensitivity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, current LLM-as-Enhancer frameworks remain complex yet underperform under low-to-moderate perturbations and show weaker robustness by the Norm-AUC metric. Algorithms also differ in sensitivity to deficiency composition, exposing a robustness gap that calls for more resilient approaches.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01910v1/system_pipeline.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/255;" width="476" height="255" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Overall architecture of RoGRAD. RoGRAD establishes the first iterative RAG+GCL paradigm for LLM-on-graph, replacing static one-shot augmentation with dynamic multi-round refinement.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>System Overview</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis indicates that LLM-as-Enhancer frameworks fail to deliver better robustness, and occasionally underperform simpler GNN-based augmentation. A key reason lies in the quality of the LLM-generated contents <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}=\{\hat{t}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}=\{\hat{t}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from the original attributes <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\{t_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\{t_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> tend to be semantically incoherent within the same class <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and homogeneous across different classes, due to the lack of semantic guidance.
After being encoded and propagated in GNNs, the learned representations <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibit high intra-class variance <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\text{intra}}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>intra</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\text{intra}}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> and small inter-class margins <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\text{inter}}(k,k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\text{inter}}(k,k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\text{intra}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}_{k}|}\sum_{v_{i}\in\mathcal{C}_{k}}\|\mathbf{z}_{i}-\bar{\mathbf{z}}_{k}\|_{2}^{2}\uparrow,\;m_{\text{inter}}(k,k^{\prime})=\|\bar{\mathbf{z}}_{k}-\bar{\mathbf{z}}_{k^{\prime}}\|_{2}\downarrow." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>intra</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">↑</mo><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\text{intra}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}_{k}|}\sum_{v_{i}\in\mathcal{C}_{k}}\|\mathbf{z}_{i}-\bar{\mathbf{z}}_{k}\|_{2}^{2}\uparrow,\;m_{\text{inter}}(k,k^{\prime})=\|\bar{\mathbf{z}}_{k}-\bar{\mathbf{z}}_{k^{\prime}}\|_{2}\downarrow.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, the adoption of one-shot generation produces each <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}_{i}=\mathcal{G}_{\theta}(t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}_{i}=\mathcal{G}_{\theta}(t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> with a LLM <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> in a single pass without refinement. As a result, low-quality or noisy augmentations persist.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To solve such issues, RoGRAD establishes a retrieval-augmented learning pipeline over the text-attributed graph <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E},\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E},\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> to enhance graph robustness by improving structural connectivity and semantic discriminability before downstream classification. The overall architecture of RoGRAD is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Empirical Analysis ‣ 3. GNN Robustness against Graph Deficiencies ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which employs:
(i) a <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semantic-Guided Generation Module (SGGM)</span> that iteratively synthesizes and refines augmentation samples;
(ii) a <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Graph Enrichment Stage</span> that injects generated samples into original <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to expand node coverage and features, introduce new high-confidence edges, and supplement pseudo-label supervision;
and (iii) a <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Contrastive Learning with RAG Refinement (R2CL)</span> module that regularizes node representations on randomly and LLM-perturbed contrastive views along both structure and embedding dimensions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, SGGM leverages <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce and iteratively refines initial sample drafts <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}_{i}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}_{i}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, where it retrieves top-<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> same-class neighbors from the embedding store
<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\{\mathbf{e}_{i}=\phi(t_{i})\mid v_{i}\in V\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\{\mathbf{e}_{i}=\phi(t_{i})\mid v_{i}\in V\}</annotation></semantics></math> as semantic groundings. Synthetic samples are then encoded by a Sentence-BERT encoder <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and a feedback <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\hat{t}_{i}^{(r)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\hat{t}_{i}^{(r)})</annotation></semantics></math> summarizes similarity-based diagnoses derived from comparisons between <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r)}</annotation></semantics></math> and existing nodes. Optimized samples are later merged into the initial graph.
Finally, R2CL constructs LLM-perturbed views <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain representations that enforce intra-class alignment and inter-class separation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Semantic-Guided Generation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SGGM refines the text <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of augmentation samples <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{i}\in\hat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{i}\in\hat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> into a high-quality augmentation <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> through a retrieval–diagnosis–revision pipeline driven by LLM <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that generations can be optimized at least once, compared with one-shot generation. Original attributes are encoded as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{j}=\phi(t_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{j}=\phi(t_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> and stored in an embedding memory <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\{\mathbf{e}_{j}\mid v_{j}\in V\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\{\mathbf{e}_{j}\mid v_{j}\in V\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the initial <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}_{i}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}_{i}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by prompts in Figure <a href="#A4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix D Prompts ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> is encoded as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(\hat{t}_{i}^{(0)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\hat{t}_{i}^{(0)})</annotation></semantics></math>. SGGM retrieves <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> semantically close same-class exemplars <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(r)}</annotation></semantics></math> at round <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as grounding context:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(r)}=\operatorname{TopK}_{j\in\mathcal{C}(i)}^{\pi}\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r)},\mathbf{e}_{j})=\operatorname{TopK}\!\left(\Big\langle\tfrac{\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r)}}{{\|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r)}\|_{2}}},\;\tfrac{\mathbf{e}_{j}}{{\|\mathbf{e}_{j}\|_{2}}}\Big\rangle\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>TopK</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.462em;" width="2.462em"><mi>TopK</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(r)}=\operatorname{TopK}_{j\in\mathcal{C}(i)}^{\pi}\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r)},\mathbf{e}_{j})=\operatorname{TopK}\!\left(\Big\langle\tfrac{\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r)}}{{\|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r)}\|_{2}}},\;\tfrac{\mathbf{e}_{j}}{{\|\mathbf{e}_{j}\|_{2}}}\Big\rangle\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01910v1/SEG_prompt.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/198;" width="476" height="198" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Prompts for semantic-guided generation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These exemplars are embedded into <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RAG-enhanced generation prompts</span> that instruct <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to: (1) analyze exemplars to extract category-specific terminology, methodologies, and topics; (2) generate a new text using representative terms while addressing a typical same-category problem; and (3) output the text in a strict Title–Abstract–Keywords format, illustrated by Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2. Semantic-Guided Generation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (i). The additional keywords are incorporated into the embedding with a fusion weight <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to enhance intra-class alignment. After generation, the new draft <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is then encoded as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)}=\psi(\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)}=\psi(\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+1)})</annotation></semantics></math> with main content and keywords.
Its similarity <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> against all existing nodes becomes <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathbf{x}_{j})]_{j\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathbf{x}_{j})]_{j\in V}</annotation></semantics></math>,
based on which, four diagnostic metrics: redundancy <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, class alignment <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, off-category drift <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and duplication <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> are computed:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max_{\scriptscriptstyle j\in\mathcal{C}(i)}\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathbf{x}_{j}),\;a_{i}^{(r+1)}=\sum_{\scriptscriptstyle j\in\operatorname{TopK}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathcal{C}(i)}}\tfrac{\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathbf{x}_{j})}{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>sim</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">TopK</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max_{\scriptscriptstyle j\in\mathcal{C}(i)}\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathbf{x}_{j}),\;a_{i}^{(r+1)}=\sum_{\scriptscriptstyle j\in\operatorname{TopK}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathcal{C}(i)}}\tfrac{\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathbf{x}_{j})}{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E3Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle o_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle o_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max_{\scriptscriptstyle j\notin\mathcal{C}(i)}\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathbf{x}_{j}),\;d_{i}^{(r+1)}=\max_{\scriptscriptstyle\hat{v}_{j}\in\hat{V}_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{prev}}}\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{j})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∉</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>sim</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.710em">v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.710em">V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>prev</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mi>sim</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max_{\scriptscriptstyle j\notin\mathcal{C}(i)}\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathbf{x}_{j}),\;d_{i}^{(r+1)}=\max_{\scriptscriptstyle\hat{v}_{j}\in\hat{V}_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{prev}}}\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{(r+1)},\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A critique process <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> then converts the diagnostic scores <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{i}^{(r+1)},\,a_{i}^{(r+1)},\,o_{i}^{(r+1)},\,d_{i}^{(r+1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{i}^{(r+1)},\,a_{i}^{(r+1)},\,o_{i}^{(r+1)},\,d_{i}^{(r+1)})</annotation></semantics></math>
into targeted refinement instructions.
Specifically, if <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> are high, the instruction emphasizes introducing novel elements;
if <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is low, it calls for reinforcing category-specific terms;
and if <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is high, it instructs to remove off-category terminology.
The <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feedback-based refinement prompts</span> in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2. Semantic-Guided Generation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Are LLMs Better GNN Helpers? Rethinking Robust Graph Learning under Deficiencies with Iterative Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (ii) guide <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to rewrite drafts with greater diversity compared to same-class exemplars, stricter category alignment, and minimal overlap with previously generated texts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the original text <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the retrieved exemplars <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(r+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(r+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the feedback <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+1)})</annotation></semantics></math> can be fed to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce the next draft:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+2)}=\mathcal{G}_{\theta}\big(t_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathcal{F}(\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+1)})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+2)}=\mathcal{G}_{\theta}\big(t_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(r+1)},\mathcal{F}(\hat{t}_{i}^{(r+1)})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This refinement stops when no violations remain or when the maximum round <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is reached.
The final <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}=\{\hat{t}_{i}^{(R)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}=\{\hat{t}_{i}^{(R)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with embeddings <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=\psi(\hat{t}_{i}^{(R)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=\psi(\hat{t}_{i}^{(R)})</annotation></semantics></math> is then utilized to enrich the initial graph.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Graph Enrichment Against Deficiencies</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Refined samples are incorporated to compensate for deficiencies with encoded features <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assemble them as <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{X}=\big[\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{1};\ldots;\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{M}\big]\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{X}=\big[\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{1};\ldots;\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{M}\big]\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and introduce the corresponding nodes <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{V}=\{\hat{v}_{1},\ldots,\hat{v}_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{V}=\{\hat{v}_{1},\ldots,\hat{v}_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math> into the original graph <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G=(V,E,X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G=(V,E,X)}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding an enriched graph <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}=(\widetilde{\mathcal{V}},\widetilde{\mathcal{E}},\widetilde{\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}=(\widetilde{\mathcal{V}},\widetilde{\mathcal{E}},\widetilde{\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{V}}=V\cup\hat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∪</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{V}}=V\cup\hat{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{X}}=[X;\hat{X}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(N+M)\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{X}}=[X;\hat{X}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(N+M)\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For structural enrichment, each <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected to semantically similar original nodes according to feature similarity <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j})=\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j})=\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The threshold <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-based neighborhood is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(\hat{v}_{i})=\{\,v_{j}\in V\mid\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j})&gt;\tau\,\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(\hat{v}_{i})=\{\,v_{j}\in V\mid\mathrm{sim}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j})&gt;\tau\,\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}\;\;\leftarrow\mathcal{E}\cup\{\,(\hat{v}_{i},v_{j})\mid v_{j}\in\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(\hat{v}_{i})\,\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0.838em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}\;\;\leftarrow\mathcal{E}\cup\{\,(\hat{v}_{i},v_{j})\mid v_{j}\in\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(\hat{v}_{i})\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For supervision enrichment, each <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned a hard pseudo-label <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}\in\{1,\dots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}\in\{1,\dots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math> aligned with its generation category, and the label set is updated as <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=[Y_{\text{orig}};\hat{Y}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=[Y_{\text{orig}};\hat{Y}]</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure their participation in optimization, we extend the training mask as <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\mathbf{m}}^{\text{train}}=[\mathbf{m}^{\text{train}};\mathbf{1}_{M}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\mathbf{m}}^{\text{train}}=[\mathbf{m}^{\text{train}};\mathbf{1}_{M}]</annotation></semantics></math> while keeping <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\mathbf{m}}^{\text{val}}=[\mathbf{m}^{\text{val}};\mathbf{0}_{M}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mtext>val</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mtext>val</mtext></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\mathbf{m}}^{\text{val}}=[\mathbf{m}^{\text{val}};\mathbf{0}_{M}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\mathbf{m}}^{\text{te}}=[\mathbf{m}^{\text{te}};\mathbf{0}_{M}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mtext>te</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mtext>te</mtext></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\mathbf{m}}^{\text{te}}=[\mathbf{m}^{\text{te}};\mathbf{0}_{M}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of generated nodes newly inserted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, the graph evolves from <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|=N+M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|=N+M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{E}|=|E|+\sum_{i}|\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(\hat{v}_{i})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{E}|=|E|+\sum_{i}|\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(\hat{v}_{i})|</annotation></semantics></math>, providing denser connectivity and richer supervisory signals with additional node embeddings. This enrichment effectively alleviates weak graph information to yield a structurally and semantically informative augmented graph.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01910v1/Contrastive_learning_prompt.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/118;" width="476" height="118" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Prompts for retrieval-refined contrastive learning.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Overall performance: (a) Improvement over Top 4 baselines on non-deficient graphs, (b) Performance under deficiencies.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:49.4pt;vertical-align:-23.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-254.7pt,36.5pt) scale(0.403750438774351,0.403750438774351) ;">
<table id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Arch.</span></th>
